00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.370 align:start position:0%
 
this morning we continued our promise to  feature at least one new book a week  during the pandemic today an exclusive  about the biggest search in the history  of the FBI and the capture of the most  wanted man in America our focus to  authors and a former agent who changed  the course of history the target James  Whitey Bulger the infamous Bob Boston  mobster whose much-discussed story was  largely incomplete until now what you're  about to see release for the first time  are the last known photos of Whitey  Bulger along with a more accurate story  of what happened while he was on the run  and after he was caught plus one final  remaining mystery the last known photo  of Whitey Bulger alive is hardly in  fitting with his menacing persona  it's him in prison with his Native  American art teacher another with the  inmates he befriended miles away from  decades of murder mayhem and the most  exhaustive manhunt in FBI history the  full story now told in the new book  hunting whitey by dave wedge and Casey  Sherman as a writer you know Dave and I  have read everything that has been  written about Whitey Bulger and his life  of crime but what about the 16 years  that he was a fugitive that story has  not been told what was his life like  behind bars the hero of this story is a  former agent who was running the case  when Bulger was captured marine Gleeson  hasn't spoken on-camera about any of it  since she left the bureau seven years  ago when you opened the book for the  first time your name is the first two  words in the book your reaction I had to  close it why did you close it there were  so many people involved in this case and  it just made me a little uncomfortable  that's all I eventually picked it back  up a couple days later and started  reading it and thought this is pretty  good  James whitey Bulger's life of crime that  began in the 1950s eventually put him in  charge of Boston's Winter Hill Gang  for decades he infamously manipulated  FBI agent John Connolly trading  information and misinformation  Bulger's been portrayed loosely by Jack  Nicholson in 2006's The Departed  and directly by Johnny Depp in 2015's  black mass get the FBI to fight he was  about to be indicted and sent away when  he slipped away on Christmas Eve 1994  and remained a fugitive for more than 16  years how did he stay on the run for  that long that was the thing that drove  me crazy when I got to Boston people  thought that we did not want to catch  him because we were afraid of what he  might say if we did catch them and there  couldn't be anything further from the  truth the thing that really disturbed me  the most was he was like a myth it was  almost had like a John Gotti like Robin  Hood type rapport with the neighborhood  and Southie that he grew up in and that  just was never an OK thing for me I knew  people who knew him who talked about him  and and there is that reverence for many  of them still yeah I don't it didn't  work well I'm like this guy is you know  a stone-cold killer Noreen Gleason  helped solve the case by changing the  game focused not on Bulger but his  girlfriend Catherine Greig we really  never took a look at Catherine I think a  lot of people thought that lady had  killed her and I was like yeah I don't  know I don't think so he needs women in  his life he was very dependent on women  the FBI is trying something new to track  down one of its most wanted suspects is  an announcement it's hope someone will  recognize the longtime girlfriend a  fugitive Boston mob boss James Whitey  Bulger who's been the book is called  hunting whitey right I hunt and fish up  here all the time but if I went hunting  and I kept hunting every day for years  doing the same thing but never was able  to catch my prey  why would I continue to do it that way  so this was a great opportunity it's  just kind of flip the switch a little  bit you weren't hunting whitey then you  were hunting Catherine if you know how  to hunt you can always find what you're  after if he just changed the strategy  she recognized that for so long the FBI  and other law enforcement agencies were  hunting an elderly white man in  sunglasses and a Red Sox hat now I can  walk down the street in Boston and spot  five of those guys right away and Noreen  Gleason said were focused too much on  him let's focus on his girlfriend and  that broke the case wide open we had  captured one of the FBI's ten most  wanted fugitives the ads that Gleason  put together led to a tip from a woman  from Iceland of all places who once knew  Greg is the woman who fed stray cats  when a tipster Greg and Bolger all lived  in the same Santa Monica California  neighborhood at approximately 5:45 p.m.  Pacific Standard time using a ruse  agents and other task force members  lured mr. Bulger out of his apartment I  think that he hid in plain sight  he really was a very disciplined person  he covered his tracks very well he knew  evasion techniques so I think that he  did a pretty good job but at the end we  were better after Bulger was sent to  prison the first attempt on his life was  made in less than a month he was stabbed  in the head and almost died during  recovery he formed a friendship with an  art teacher Clement chip Janis wedge and  Sherman reveal the dozens of letters the  two exchanged and the bond they shared  this photo is I mean it's interesting to  look at it's right it's not what you'd  expect to see of the last known photo of  what evolves you know every every  surveillance photo of Bulger he's  scowling and it's shot from a distance  here's Bulger behind prison walls  smiling and it shows that the pressure  was kind of off at that time he didn't  have to look over his shoulder anymore  to get captured he did to protect his  life and he would tell this fellow  prisoner when I get it I hope they give  it to me quick because I gave it quick  Bulger was eventually murdered in prison  with a padlock and sock less than 12  hours after mysteriously being  transferred to a West Virginia facility  in 2018 the main suspect is a former  mobster who answered to La Cosa Nostra  why was whitey killed in prison  one is that he's a notorious rat he's  one of the biggest most infamous rats  ever in the history of organized crime  he put a lot of people in jail his  actions put a lot of people in the  morgue  specifically Italian mobsters Italian  mobsters don't forget stuff like that no  one has officially been charged in that  case but that open crime is far from the  largest open question  when Bulger was captured he was found  with a cache of weapons and $800,000  hidden behind his apartments walls  Bulger however spent decades stuffing  away millions more in safety deposit  boxes all over the country do you think  we'll ever find out where all that money  is I think that people at know Bulger  know where the money is and the FBI  agents that we interviewed for the book  said follow some of those people around  because eventually they're going to lead  you to the lottery if there's millions  of dollars still missing where is it and  do you think it ever is found if those  are assets that can still be seized  somehow and people are continuously  working on this then they'll come up  with it you think it'll happen people  thought that we would never get him so  I'm always very optimistic when it comes  to that today as the search for the  missing millions continues Bulger is  buried in a cemetery in West Roxbury  Massachusetts  Whitey's name is not on the grave when  people think about Whitey Bulger today  what should they think good riddance  Dana Katherine Craig whitey Bulger's  girlfriend is now out of prison she is  living in the Boston area why is it  important to find all that money if it's  out there well that money would go to  victims and victims families those who  were affected by whitey Bulger's crimes  I cannot wait to read that book that is  fascinating absolutely fascinating was  already interested yeah great great job  Jeff

